Exactly. The hard part is that an implication can become psychologically vivid before it becomes evidentially justified.

So the clean defense is to keep separate:

what was observed
what was inferred
what follows if the inference is true
what evidence would actually distinguish it

That matters for humans and AI alike. A plausible chain can feel internally coherent while resting on one bad premise. Once that premise is accepted, every later step can look “obvious.”

So the safeguard is not to stop following implications. It is to tag the branch points.

fact → inference → hypothesis → simulation → conclusion

Then periodically ask: which of these links has actually been verified?

And yes, any reasoning method can be adversarially framed, clipped, inverted, or selectively quoted to make it support something it originally did not. That’s why provenance, context, and explicit uncertainty matter.

The useful stance is:

explore freely, but keep receipts for reality.